{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on request 598f0d03, identifiers (c+d): yes. The producer of a\nretained use comes from the existing retained row. This corrects item\n1a of ruling a94bfa4d, which said that it comes from the entry.\n\n1. The fact, the content digest and the kind come from the FactUse in\n   the local `Own` entry, and from nowhere else.\n2. The bytes and the producer come from the one retained row that the\n   scope's own store holds for that content. The constructor checks\n   the digest of the bytes against the content of the use, as before.\n3. The store wrote that producer in the commit that sealed the entry,\n   and `retainedFacts` and `Operations.#record` read the same column\n   today. So the trust does not change. The type says that the\n   producer is the store's record beside the bytes, and that the entry\n   does not hash it.\n4. No member joins FactUse or the entry, and no caller supplies a\n   producer. A use with no retained row, or with bytes of another\n   digest, makes no retained use.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}